%S A399124 1,2,2,6,2,52,2,394,1766,3530,2,461738,2,368084,72144074,581242378,2,
%T A399124 15365378602,2,5530398496066,3840162697802,6911587594,2,
%U A399124 40421348949401034,491609948246960402,1103800820002,343314385346017802,373869379947072011402,2
%N A399124 Number of permutations pi of [n] such that LIS(pi)*LDS(pi) = n, where LIS and LDS are the lengths of the longest increasing and decreasing subsequences.
%C A399124 Under the Robinson-Schensted correspondence, let lambda be the common shape of the two standard Young tableaux associated with pi. Then LIS(pi) is the length of the first row of lambda and LDS(pi) is the number of rows of lambda. Since the Young diagram of lambda has n cells and lies inside the rectangle having these dimensions, LIS(pi)*LDS(pi)=n if and only if lambda is rectangular. A rectangle with r rows and s columns, r*s=n, contributes T(r,s)^2 permutations, where T(r,s) is the number of standard Young tableaux of that rectangular shape.
%C A399124 The factorizations are counted with order: for r != s, the rectangle with r rows and s columns and its transpose are distinct shapes. Equivalently, a(n)/n! is the probability, under Plancherel measure on partitions of n, that the partition is rectangular.
%H A399124 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A399124/b399124.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..100</a>
%H A399124 J. S. Frame, G. de B. Robinson, and R. M. Thrall, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1954-030-1">The hook graphs of the symmetric group</a>, Canadian Journal of Mathematics 6 (1954), 316-324.
%H A399124 Dan Romik, <a href="https://danromik.com/resources/books/the-surprising-mathematics-of-longest-increasing-subsequences.pdf">The Surprising Mathematics of Longest Increasing Subsequences</a>, Cambridge University Press, 2015.
%H A399124 C. Schensted, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1961-015-3">Longest increasing and decreasing subsequences</a>, Canadian Journal of Mathematics 13 (1961), 179-191.
%H A399124 Richard P. Stanley, <a href="https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/pubs/pubfiles/142.pdf">Increasing and decreasing subsequences and their variants</a>, ICM Madrid 2006, Vol. I, 545-579.
%H A399124 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plancherel_measure">Plancherel measure</a>.
%H A399124 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinson%E2%80%93Schensted_correspondence">Robinson-Schensted correspondence</a>.
%F A399124 a(n) = Sum_{r|n} T(r,n/r)^2 where T(r,s) = A321716(max(r,s),min(r,s)) = (r*s)! * Product_{j=0..r-1} (j!/(s+j)!), the number of standard Young tableaux of the rectangle with r rows and s columns.
%F A399124 If p is prime, then a(p)=2.
%F A399124 If p is prime, then a(p^2)=A079402(p)+2.
%e A399124 For n=6 the rectangular shapes are (6), (3,3), (2,2,2), and (1^6), with 1, 5, 5, and 1 standard Young tableaux, respectively; hence a(6)=1^2+5^2+5^2+1^2=52.
%t A399124 A399124[n_Integer] := Total[Function[r, With[{s = n/r}, With[{val = ((n! * BarnesG[r + 1] * BarnesG[s + 1]) / BarnesG[r + s + 1])^2}, If[r == s, val, 2*val]]]] /@ Select[Divisors[n], # <= Sqrt[n] &]]; Table[A399124[n], {n, 1, 29}]  (* _Peter Luschny_, Sep 01 2026 *)
%o A399124 (Python)  # Using function A321716.
%o A399124 from math import isqrt
%o A399124 def a(n: int) -> int:
%o A399124     total = 0
%o A399124     for r in range(1, isqrt(n) + 1):
%o A399124         s, m = divmod(n, r)
%o A399124         if m == 0:
%o A399124             val = A321716(r, s) ** 2
%o A399124             total += val if r == s else val + val
%o A399124     return total
%o A399124 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 30)])  # _Peter Luschny_, Aug 31 2026
%Y A399124 Cf. A060854 (standard Young tableaux of a fixed rectangular shape), A067228 (sum of their numbers over rectangular shapes of size n), A079402 (square-shape contribution), A321275 (sum of LIS*LDS over all permutations), A321313 (permutations with LIS=LDS), A321716.
